[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m user
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m You have seven bags of gold coins. Each bag has the same number of gold coins. One day, you find a bag of 53 coins. You decide to redistribute the number of coins you have so that all eight bags you hold have the same number of coins. You successfully manage to redistribute all the coins, and you also note that you have more than 200 coins. What is the smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins?
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m output:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m Let's denote the number of coins in each of the original seven bags as \( x \). Therefore, the total number of coins before finding the 53rd coin is \( 7x \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m When you find the 53rd coin, you have \( 7x + 53 \) coins in total. After redistributing the coins so that all eight bags have the same number of coins, each bag will have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \frac{7x + 53}{8} \text{ coins}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m Since this is an integer, \( 7x + 53 \) must be divisible by 8. We can express this condition as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 7x + 53 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m Simplifying \( 7x + 53 \equiv 0 \pmod{8} \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 7x + 53 \equiv 7x + 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{8} \implies 7x \equiv -1 \pmod{8} \implies 7x \equiv 7 \pmod{8}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m Since \( 7 \equiv -1 \pmod{8} \), we can divide both sides by 7, noting that the multiplicative inverse of 7 modulo 8 is 7 (since \( 7 \times 7 = 49 \equiv 1 \pmod{8} \)):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m x \equiv 1 \pmod{8}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m This means \( x \) can be written as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m x = 8k + 1 \text{ for some integer } k
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m The total number of coins before finding the 53rd coin is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 7x = 7(8k + 1) = 56k + 7
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m We are given that this total number of coins is more than 200:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 56k + 7 > 200 \implies 56k > 193 \implies k > \frac{193}{56} \approx 3.446
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m Since \( k \) is an integer, the smallest possible value for \( k \) is 4. Substituting \( k = 4 \) into the expression for the total number of coins:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m 56 \cdot 4 + 7 = 224 + 7 = 231
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m Thus, the smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \boxed{231}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1952501)[0m \]
